00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:05.289 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  the day I got an offer to be a  consultant after college a good friend  told me girl you're done the hardest  part is getting the job that got me  really excited but I also heard this  anxious voice speaking inside my head  that voice is my inner critic who I call  Nancy and Nancy kept telling me Renata I  don't think it should be that excited  you have zero consulting skills Nancy  was right but she calmed down after all  of the training that every consultant  has to get through a few years later  when I met my wonderful partner John I  had a strangely similar experience  although my friends told me you're done  the hardest parade is finding the right  person and I was really excited but  Nancy came back screaming Renata what do  you know about relationships  clearly not much because so far a  hundred percent of them have and it  I know but before I tell you what  happened next I want to take a quick  poll raise your hand if you think that  love is a pretty important part of life  everyone all of us great now raise your  hand if you feel like you've been really  well educated on how to love  none of us let's think about that for a  second we all value our careers and if  you're relatively well educated on how  to succeed that's how we got here but  when it comes to love which overwhelming  research points as a top predictor of  life satisfaction health and wealth we  kind of just wait or swipe until we find  the right person we look at the couples  around us we watch what they do right  and wrong and we assume we know how to  do right but that's like hoping to  become a doctor by watching Grey's  Anatomy and that's what I was thinking  about when I met John I felt like I  finally met the right person but I  didn't feel educated on how to be the  right person and I started getting  really scared that I would eventually  mess everything up and I really didn't  want to mess anything up so I started  reading every piece of research in every  book that I could find about the science  of love and relationships luckily John  loves reading so he followed me down the  rabbit hole  which is very convenient but also a  little weird now now we talk about our  relationship and feelings in terms of  stuff like attachment theory and the  neurobiology of desire and emotions and  we haven't even taken touchy-feely even  though it might not be sexy to talk  about your relationship in scientific  terms it's kind of like having food in  your teeth you really want to know so  you can do something about it I have to  say though I try to be really discreet  about my interest in relationship topics  for a long time I felt like it was too  personal controversial and frankly too  embarrassing to discuss with anyone else  bye John  but then one day my brother Rodrigo call  me to ask hey is everything okay between  you and John and I said yeah what's  going on  well my brother and I happen to share an  Amazon account which sometimes gets  awkward he was calling because he  noticed the trend in my recent book  orders seven principles for making  marriage work your brain unloved mating  in captivity and many many other  relationship hooks the John and I read  together we don't do Netflix and chill  we read and chill now when my brother  called me he was also getting an MBA so  I told him listen we're both getting an  MBA because it's valuable but it's not  always been valuable when the MBA was  created a hundred years ago people  thought it was crazy the business was  being elevated to the same level of  expertise as law and engineering now  it's the most popular degree in the u.s.  we finally see value in business  education and we have to do the same for  love now am I suggesting you create a  formal master's degree and love no but I  am suggesting we all will start seeing  ourselves as CEOs of our own love lives  we know from research that couples to  thrive and couples who fail behave very  differently we also know from research  that just like in business there is no  formula but there are tools and just  like great CEOs couples who thrive  master tools that make them better every  day today I'm gonna share my top three  favorites like a good consultant number  one choose to see love as a skill by far  the most important thing that I've  learned from this journey is that love  is not something that happens to us it's  the skill we can choose to learn  practice and master over time  it's not easy and will never be perfect  but seeing it as a skill is a first step  to getting better number two choose  generosity over judgment if you've been  the parent of a child or a puppy you  know what it feels like to see really  unpleasant behavior with generosity not  judgment you understand that they are  still growing up but if our partner does  something we don't like we judge we get  offended they should be fully grown up  by now but as long as we're alive we're  all still growing up it's easy to love  the wonderful things about a partner but  the true love skill is seeing what's not  so wonderful about them with generosity  not judgment number 3 choose to become  an expert on your partner we know from  neuroscience that if you take couples  who report to still be madly in love  after 25 years and put them through an  MRI machine when you show them a photo  of their partner their brain lights up  just like couples who just fell in love  which is crazy because that basically  means that the honeymoon phase doesn't  have to be over at the honeymoon couples  who stay in love are couples who stay  curious  every day they commit to learning about  each other like an ax breed they never  stop learning it's a bit ironic that we  find it romantic to say things like oh  my god I know everything about my  boyfriend personally I find him much  more romantic to think about how much  more I want to know about my partner and  how much more of an expert I want to  become every day now these tools are not  a checklist they're a lifelong practice  but it's easy to forget that when we get  busy with life so I want to leave you  with something that helps me remind  remind myself of being accountable every  day both in business and in love as CEOs  in love or business we're all given two  options every single day you can either  blame external conditions for your  performance or you can take radical  responsibility for practicing the skills  that make you better now the question is  which one will you choose  [Applause]  [Music]

